module lib/hf/md_eri
  The electron repulsion integrals (ERIs) of a basis, by the
  McMurchie-Davidson method (lib/hf/hermite): the numbers
  $(ab|cd) = \iint \phi_a(r_1)\phi_b(r_1)\, \frac{1}{|r_1 - r_2|}\, \phi_c(r_2)\phi_d(r_2)$
  from which the SCF builds its Coulomb and exchange matrices J and K.

  The work is organized by shell group: the shells of one general
  contraction (same atom, same angular momentum, same exponents, as
  Dunning's sets have them) form a group, and their primitive integrals are
  computed once for all of them. A pair of groups (the bra or the ket) is
  prepared once, with the exponent $p$, the center $P$ and the E
  coefficients of each of its primitive pairs; a shell quartet (a bra pair and
  a ket pair) is then two matrix products over those prepared E values and
  one R table per primitive quartet. Quartets whose Schwarz bound
  $\sqrt{(ab|ab)(cd|cd)}$ is below a threshold are skipped, since every
  integral they hold is smaller than it. The result is the packed vector of
  lib/hf/eri_store, which stores each distinct integral once.

mderi_packsize  @46f3a13e
  why   The store is one flat array, so each group pair's pack must be sized
        before it is filled.
  la    the first group's angular momentum
  lb    the second group's angular momentum
  npp   the number of primitive pairs
  ca    the shells in the first group
  cb    the shells in the second group

mderi_fillpack  @40d048a0
  why   Everything about a group pair that does not depend on the other pair
        of a quartet: computed once here and read by every quartet the pair
        is in. The contraction coefficients, the component factors and the
        product prefactor go into the E entries, so the quartet code only
        multiplies matrices. The shells of a group share their exponents, so
        one primitive pair's E table serves every shell pair of the two
        groups; each shell pair gets rows of its own with its own
        contraction coefficients, and the R tables of a quartet are computed
        once for all of them.
  ST      the store being filled
  off     where this pair's pack starts in `ST`
  atoms   the atom pack: charge and position, `[natom, AT_LEN]`
  shells  the shell pack
  prims   the primitive pack
  cn      the contracted coefficients from bas_contract
  sa      the first group's first shell
  nga     the shells in the first group
  sb      the second group's first shell
  ngb     the shells in the second group
  ga      a shell of the first group, counted from sa
  gb      a shell of the second group, counted from sb
  r0      the first row of the shell pair (ga, gb)
  la      the first shell's angular momentum; lb the second's
  na      the first shell's Cartesian component count; nb the second's
  npa     the first shell's primitive count; npb the second's
  npp     the primitive pairs, `npa npb`
  L       `la + lb`, the pair's total angular momentum
  nh      the Hermite functions of order up to `L`
  PA      the first shell's component powers; PB the second's
  H       the Hermite index list $(t, u, v)$
  NA      the component factors of the first shell; NB the second's
  Ax      the first shell's atom's $x$; the other five coordinates likewise
  EX      the E table on $x$ for the current primitive pair; EY and EZ on $y$, $z$
  nt      the $t$ values per $(i, j)$ in an E table
  ld      the $j$ values per $i$
  ncol    the columns of $E_{ab}$, `npp nh`
  e0      where $E_{ab}$ starts in the pack
  pa0     the first shell's first primitive row; pb0 the second's
  a       the first primitive's exponent; b the second's
  k       the primitive pair's index
  p       $p = a + b$, the product's exponent
  c       the product of the two shells' contraction coefficients
  ox      where E(i, j, .) of this component pair starts on $x$; oy and oz likewise
  tx      the highest $t$ with a nonzero E on $x$; ty and tz likewise
  w       the coefficient and component factors of this entry
  rowoff  where this component pair's row, for this primitive pair, starts

  = NA#2      the component factors, one per component, so each shell shares one set of coefficients
  = ST#6      the primitive pair's exponent $p$, then its center $P = (aA + bB)/p$
  = c         after herm_efill has filled this primitive pair's E table on each axis: the product of the two shells' contraction coefficients, for each shell pair of the groups
  = ST#10     one entry of $E_{ab}$: the product of the three axes' E values, zero beyond $t \le i + j$ on each axis

