mderi_groups  @4881e370
  why   The basis tables write a general contraction as segmented shells one
        after the other (lib/basis), so the groups are runs of neighbors that
        share their primitives. Carbon in cc-pVDZ has two s contractions
        over the same eight exponents: grouped, a quartet of those shells
        computes its primitive integrals once instead of up to sixteen times
        [test "the grouped store gives the segmented store's integrals with fewer pairs, cc-pVDZ water"].
  shells  the shell pack
  prims   the primitive pack
  nsh     the number of shells
  first   the first shell of each group
  ng      the number of groups
  GT      the groups, `[ng, 2]`
  s       a shell
  g       a group
  nxt     the first shell after group g

